specs indexes a specification corpus and reports conformance findings against the rules the active profile registers. Configuration is read from the nearest specs.toml in the working directory or a directory above it, searching up to the repository root, or from the file --config names.

Usage:
  specs [command]

Available Commands:
  c4          Emit the corpus's C4 model: elements, relationships and excluded statements
  check       Check the corpus, or a single document, for conformance
  criterion   Query an acceptance criterion, or list ones that changed locally
  feature     Query a feature, or emit one as a standalone Gherkin file
  headings    Report the headings matching a selection, with the span of each one's section
  help        Help about any command
  index       Build the spec index and write it to disk
  links       Rewrite a document's wikilinks into markdown links
  list        Report every corpus member's path, slug, type, status, title and description
  next-id     Return the next identifier for a numbered document type
  refs        Report which corpus members reference a slug or one of its headings, or what it references
  slug-exists Report whether a corpus member declares the given slug

Flags:
      --config string   path to specs.toml (default: the nearest specs.toml in the working directory or above it, up to the repository root)
  -h, --help            help for specs
      --version         print the build's version, revision, time and modified state as YAML

Use "specs [command] --help" for more information about a command.
